  "positioning": "Strong lane on portfolio, weaker on credentials, which is the usual shape for production work and it is fine because this lane hires on reel. The reel is the application. Lead with Reels for ad-creative and social roles, and with the scripted channel work for anything narrative or content-led. The Unreal Engine work is genuinely unusual for a junior video candidate and worth surfacing wherever a posting touches real-time, virtual production or game content.\n\nThe gap worth naming is crewed production: he has been a one-person operation, so he has not worked as part of a set or a post team with a producer, a schedule and someone else's brief. Most in-scope postings grade Tier A.\n\nOne standing caution carried from lane 7. Do not let this lane's strength pull marketing applications toward camera work. A video-first framing on a digital marketing posting costs him the 11202 mapping and reads as the wrong hire.",
  "interview_watch": "Expect a reel review and expect to be asked what you would change about a piece in it. Pick the honest answer rather than defending the work. Expect a turnaround question: how fast can you cut a Reel from raw footage. Have a real number.",
  "pr_note": "Both codes are TEER 2 and clear the floor. No RCIP list covers either, so never tag a lane 9 role RCIP. AIP covers them from a designated Atlantic employer. Ontario's Workforce Priority stream takes them with a qualifying offer.",
  "not_required": "Feature-film credits, union membership, a film degree. A posting requiring any of them is a credential-gate drop.",
  "notes": [
    {
      "heading": "Intro",
      "text": "NOC 52119 or 52110, TEER 2. Track 2. Read 00-index.md for the Status conventions and tier rules."
    }
